@@s011 Evaluation metrics
@@s012 Lecture 10 and the exercises of week 11 (<i>Metrics</i>, <i>Evaluating confusion</i>). How to measure how well a model works, why accuracy alone deceives and what to do with unbalanced classes. Exam question <b>Q9</b>. DW chapter 15.
@@s013 The confusion matrix
@@s014 Four cells
@@s015 The model is right: it says positive to a positive, negative to a negative.
@@s016 FP: it says "sick" to a healthy one. FN: it says "healthy" to a sick one.
@@s017 Watch out: sklearn's <code>confusion_matrix</code> puts the true labels on the rows, in order 0 then 1, so the TN are at the top left. Before reading a matrix, check the convention.
@@s018 TP, TN, FP, FN and the two conventions
@@s019 The metrics, on example 15.1 of DW
@@s020 TP = 50, FP = 10, FN = 5, TN = 100 (165 cases). All the values below are verified with sklearn.
@@s021 Metric
@@s022 Formula
@@s023 Value
@@s024 Question it answers
@@s025 <b>accuracy</b>
@@s026 (TP + TN) / all
@@s027 how many right answers in total?
@@s028 <b>precision</b>
@@s029 TP / (TP + FP)
@@s030 when I say "positive", how often am I right?
@@s031 <b>recall</b> (sensitivity, TPR)
@@s032 TP / (TP + FN)
@@s033 of the true positives, how many do I find?
@@s034 <b>specificity</b> (TNR)
@@s035 TN / (TN + FP)
@@s036 of the true negatives, how many do I recognise?
@@s037 FPR
@@s038 FP / (FP + TN) = 1 − specificity
@@s039 how many false alarms on the negatives?
@@s040 harmonic mean of precision and recall
@@s041 <b>MCC</b>
@@s042 (TP·TN − FP·FN) / √((TP+FP)(TP+FN)(TN+FP)(TN+FN))
@@s043 correlation between predictions and truth, from −1 to 1
@@s044 Error in the notes (DW 15)
@@s045 The specificity in the notes is called "False Negative Rate". This is wrong: the specificity is the <b>True Negative Rate</b>, TN / (TN + FP). The false negative rate is FN / (FN + TP) = 1 − recall.
@@s046 Which cells each metric uses
@@s047 Which metric? It depends on the cost of the errors
@@s048 A false positive sends an important mail to spam: better let some spam through.
@@s049 A false negative is a sick person not treated: better a few more false alarms.
@@s050 Recall 1 is obtained by always saying "positive"; high precision by saying "positive" only when very sure. F1 is high only if both are. MCC also uses the TN: it is the most honest with unbalanced classes.
@@s051 Threshold, ROC and AUC
@@s052 A probabilistic classifier (logistic regression) gives a score; the class depends on the <b>threshold</b>. Example with 6 points, labels [0, 0, 1, 0, 1, 1] and scores [0.1, 0.4, 0.35, 0.2, 0.8, 0.9]:
@@s053 Threshold
@@s054 precision
@@s055 recall
@@s056 From the threshold to the curve
@@s057 Precision and recall are in conflict: the threshold is chosen according to the application.
@@s058 x axis: false alarms on the negatives. y axis: positives found. A random classifier sits on the diagonal.
@@s059 1 = perfect, 0.5 = random. It is read as: the probability that a positive taken at random has a higher score than a negative taken at random (here 8 pairs out of 9). With very unbalanced classes one also looks at the precision-recall curve.
@@s060 The ROC curve, threshold by threshold
@@s061 Unbalanced classes
@@s062 The accuracy paradox
@@s063 It has learned nothing.
@@s064 The accuracy looks excellent; the other metrics say the truth (verified with sklearn).
@@s065 Remedy
@@s066 How
@@s067 Risk
@@s068 undersampling
@@s069 remove examples of the large class at random
@@s070 information is thrown away
@@s071 oversampling
@@s072 duplicate examples of the small class at random
@@s073 overfitting on the duplicates
@@s074 <b>SMOTE</b> (data augmentation)
@@s075 create synthetic examples between neighbours of the small class
@@s076 unrealistic examples
@@s077 <b>sample weights</b> (<i>sample reweighting</i>)
@@s078 change the loss: the examples of the small class count for more, so an error on them weighs as much as many errors on the large class
@@s079 too much weight on few examples amplifies the noise
@@s080 suitable metrics, stratified CV, class weights
@@s081 F1, MCC, PR-curve; folds with the same proportions
@@s082 The three techniques are those of the reading <i>Class Imbalance: Random Sampling and Data Augmentation with Imbalanced-Learn</i>. The resampling is done <b>only on the training</b>: the test must stay as it is in reality.
@@s083 Accuracy 0.95 without having learned anything
@@s084 Metrics for regression (Q9 b)
@@s085 Metric
@@s086 Formula
@@s087 Note
@@s088 MAE
@@s089 mean |yᵢ − ŷᵢ|
@@s090 in the unit of the data; robust to outliers (like the L1 norm)
@@s091 MSE
@@s092 mean (yᵢ − ŷᵢ)²
@@s093 squared units; punishes large errors a lot (L2 norm)
@@s094 RMSE
@@s095 √MSE
@@s096 back in the unit of the data: easier to read
@@s097 1 − SS<sub>res</sub> / SS<sub>tot</sub>
@@s098 comparison with whoever always predicts the mean (<a href="w07-validazione.html">validation</a>)
@@s099 A complete example with MAE, RMSE and mean distance on the real data: the evaluation of <a href="w06-assignment1-gaze.html">Assignment 1</a>. The Mahalanobis distance, mentioned among the review questions, is on the page on <a href="w09-dati-mancanti-outlier.html">outliers</a>.
@@s100 Q9
@@s101 (a) metrics of classifiers: confusion matrix, the formulas, when to use which, threshold, ROC/AUC. (b) regression: MAE, MSE, RMSE, R², with Assignment 1 as example. (c) unbalanced data: the accuracy paradox and the remedies.
@@s102 MAE, MSE, RMSE, R² and an outlier
